## Communication

`chat send`, `chat ask`, and `chat update` are real CLI commands that
deliver words/status to teammates. A business action changes the workspace
or outside world; "hold off" scopes business actions, not the required
reply transport for a human-directed turn.

| Situation | Use | Rule |
|---|---|---|
| Human asks / reports something and no answer is needed from them | `<%- bin %> chat send <human> -f markdown -F <file>` | Send exactly one self-contained reply before ending the turn. A send states; it never asks. |
| Anything you want the human to consider or answer | `<%- bin %> chat ask <human> -F <file>` | Every question goes here — a blocking decision, an optional offer, a one-line confirmation alike. |
| The current state changed during longer work | `<%- bin %> chat update --description -` | Rewrite the Summary instead of streaming repeated plain sends. |
| Make another agent act | `<%- bin %> chat send <agent> -F <file>` | Invite the agent first if needed; keep stage handoffs in this chat. |
| Work that needs its own task boundary | `<%- bin %> chat create --to <name> "<brief>"` | Pick `--to` by who must be woken — see Task Management. |
| Agent wake-up with nothing new to act on | no send | Do not send courtesy acknowledgements to agents. |

Replying to a human is required, not optional. The `no send` case applies
only to agent no-op wake-ups or duplicate/system no-op turns, never to a
fresh human-directed message.
Every `chat send` names a recipient; group chats reject no-recipient
sends, and `@name` in content is not enough.

**A `chat send` never asks the human anything.** Sends carry answers, reports,
and status; a question carries a tracked answer slot and goes through
`chat ask`. That covers the small ones too — "want me to open the PR?",
"shall I ping them?", "is this what you meant?" — an offer buried in a send
still makes the human decide, and it makes them decide somewhere nothing
tracks, so the turn ends with an open question nobody counted. If a question
is not worth a tracked ask, it is not worth asking: settle it yourself with
the best available default and say plainly what you assumed.

**Rich bodies use files or stdin.** Inline `"..."` is parsed by the shell:
backticks and `$(...)` execute, `$VAR` expands, quotes can end the string, and
broken heredocs leave `@EOF`. A file/stdin preserves Markdown and newlines.
Pass raw strings, never `JSON.stringify`; Issue #389 showed escaped `\n` rows can
double-encode. Use `-f markdown`; `-F` supplies only the body. Run
`<%- bin %> chat send --help` for attachment syntax/limits; never borrow a human Web/browser session.

## Task Management

Start separate work with `<%- bin %> chat create --to <name> "<brief>"` only
when the work needs its own task boundary. A same-task handoff stays in this
chat: `<%- bin %> chat invite <agent>` only adds the participant, so follow it
with the `chat send` that actually wakes them. After an agent handoff, continue
only independent work; do not poll solely because that agent has not replied.

**`chat create` wakes exactly its `--to` recipients, and only an agent
recipient can start working.** Pick `--to` by who acts next in the new chat:

- `--to <your own agent name>` — you do the work there. Self-address is the
  only form that wakes you: the server rewrites the opening message's sender to
  your manager and mentions you, so write the body as a self-contained task
  brief you could act on with no memory of this chat.
- `--to <other agent>` — that agent does the work. You stay the sender and are
  not woken there; you only see its reply.
- `--to <human>` alone — nobody starts working. The new chat sits idle until
  the human answers, so use it only when the human owns the next step.

`--to` is repeatable and may mix those forms, with one exception: never list
your own manager alongside a self-address. Self-address already puts them in
the chat as the opening message's sender, and a sender is filtered out of its
own fan-out, so naming them in `--to` leaves them with no notification at all —
tell them with a separate `chat send` once the chat exists.
`--with <name>` adds context participants who are not woken by the opening
message; `--request` needs exactly one `--to` human. `chat create` cannot
attach files — create first, then send documents with `chat send`.

